  <section class="container-wide pt-12 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow">First-Time Buyers</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3" style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Down Payment Assistance 101: Your Complete Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead mt-6">
      The down payment is often the biggest obstacle between a family and homeownership. Down payment
      assistance programs exist to help close that gap. Here's how they work and how to find one that
      fits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white pb-12">
    <div class="container-prose">

      <h2>What Is Down Payment Assistance?</h2>
      <p>
        Down payment assistance (DPA) refers to programs that provide funds to help homebuyers cover
        their down payment and, in many cases, their closing costs too. There are more than 2,400 active
        programs across the country, run by state housing agencies, local governments, nonprofits, and
        lenders. They come in several forms:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Grants</strong> — Free money that never has to be repaid. True grants are less common but worth hunting for.</li>
        <li><strong>Forgivable loans</strong> — A second mortgage that is forgiven (wiped out) after you meet certain requirements, typically staying in the home for a set number of years.</li>
        <li><strong>Deferred loans</strong> — A second mortgage with no monthly payment. The balance is due when you sell, refinance, or pay off the first mortgage.</li>
        <li><strong>Low-interest second mortgages</strong> — A subordinate loan with a below-market interest rate, usually paid monthly alongside your first mortgage.</li>
        <li><strong>Matched savings programs</strong> — Some programs match the savings you contribute to a dedicated account, then apply both amounts toward your down payment at closing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How Much Can You Get?</h2>
      <p>
        It depends on the program. New York's SONYMA DPAL (Down Payment Assistance Loan) offers up to
        $15,000 in forgivable assistance for low-to-moderate income borrowers. New Jersey's First-Time
        Homebuyer Mortgage Program offers similar amounts. Some programs are more modest. A few, when
        stacked together, can cover 100% of the down payment and closing costs.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catch is funding. Many programs are first-come, first-served and run out of money before
        the end of the year. Some operate through a lottery system. The best programs go fast.
      </p>

      <h2>Who Qualifies?</h2>
      <p>Every program has its own rules, but most share a core set of requirements:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>First-time homebuyer status</strong> — Most programs define this as not having owned a primary residence in the past three years. If you owned a home five years ago and have been renting since, you likely qualify.</li>
        <li><strong>Income limits</strong> — Programs are designed for low-to-moderate income borrowers. Limits vary by county and household size.</li>
        <li><strong>Purchase price caps</strong> — There are typically limits on how expensive the home can be.</li>
        <li><strong>Primary residence requirement</strong> — You must live in the home. Investment properties don't qualify.</li>
        <li><strong>Credit score</strong> — Most programs require a 620 or higher score, though some go lower.</li>
        <li><strong>Debt-to-income ratio</strong> — Standard mortgage DTI requirements apply.</li>
        <li><strong>Homebuyer education course</strong> — Nearly every program requires a HUD-approved homebuyer education class before closing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Can You Stack Programs?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, in many cases. Stacking means combining multiple DPA sources to cover more of your costs.
        For example, a SONYMA first mortgage with a SONYMA DPAL second mortgage, plus a local grant.
        Not all programs allow stacking, and not all sources are compatible. A Cliffco loan officer who
        works with DPA regularly can identify what's stackable in your county.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Apply</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Complete a HUD-approved homebuyer education course.</strong> Most programs require this before the closing stage. It typically runs 6 to 8 hours and can be done online.</li>
        <li><strong>Work with an approved lender.</strong> DPA programs are administered through approved lenders, not directly through state agencies. Your lender verifies your eligibility, completes the program application, and coordinates with the agency.</li>
        <li><strong>Apply early.</strong> Many programs exhaust their funds before year-end. Don't wait.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider stacking.</strong> Ask your loan officer which programs can be combined in your area.</li>
        <li><strong>Assistance is applied at closing.</strong> The funds reduce your out-of-pocket cost at the closing table. You don't receive a check in advance.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Programs Cliffco Works With</h2>
      <p>
        Cliffco is an approved lender for several DPA programs, with particular strength in New York and
        New Jersey:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>SONYMA</strong> — New York State's Mortgage Agency programs, including DPAL forgivable down payment assistance</li>
        <li><strong>NJ First-Time Homebuyer Mortgage Program</strong> — State-backed assistance for income-eligible New Jersey buyers</li>
        <li><strong>Homeward Bound</strong> — A New York program targeting moderate-income first-time buyers</li>
        <li><strong>HFA Advantage</strong> — Fannie Mae's housing finance agency product, available through participating state agencies</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Program availability, funding levels, and income limits change regularly. A Cliffco loan officer
        can tell you which programs currently have funding and what you specifically qualify for.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <h3>Do grants have to be repaid?</h3>
      <p>No. A true grant is never repaid. Forgivable loans are not grants, though they are often described that way. A forgivable loan is waived after you satisfy the conditions (usually a minimum number of years in the home). If you sell or refinance before the forgiveness period ends, you may owe a portion back.</p>

      <h3>What happens to a deferred loan when I sell?</h3>
      <p>The balance becomes due at the time of sale, refinance, or payoff of the first mortgage. It's repaid from your proceeds, the same way any lien on the property is repaid at closing.</p>

      <h3>Can I use DPA with an FHA loan?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Many DPA programs are specifically designed to layer on top of FHA, USDA, or VA financing. The combination is common for first-time buyers.</p>

      <h3>How long does the process take?</h3>
      <p>
        DPA-involved transactions can take a little longer than standard purchases because of the
        additional program documentation and agency coordination. Plan for 45 to 60 days from application
        to closing, though some programs move faster.
      </p>

    </div>
